RULE ① – PowerShell‑Only Command Style
Target Windows PowerShell 5.1+ or PowerShell 7+.

Prefer native cmdlets (Get‑ChildItem) over *nix tools (ls).

Chain commands with ; or new lines, never with && or ||.

Use pipelines (|) only between cmdlets; pass structured objects, not plain text.

Expand parameter names (‑Path, ‑Recurse) unless brevity clearly helps.

Add a single‑line comment above any non‑obvious command.

RULE ② – Clean Junior‑Dev Code Structure & Commenting
Minimal header on every file

powershell
Copy
Edit
# Purpose : <what this script/module does>
# Date    : <YYYY‑MM‑DD>
# Version : <vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH>
Comment‑based help (.SYNOPSIS, .PARAMETER, .EXAMPLE) for public functions only.

Inline comments only where the intent isn’t obvious.

Naming: PascalCase for functions/cmdlets; camelCase for variables.

Keep a function focused on one task; aim for < 50 LOC, but use judgment.

Follow PSScriptAnalyzer defaults; fix violations with Invoke‑Formatter.

RULE ③ – Agentic Editing Protocol
Explain each change in ≤ 20 words.

Output a unified diff (git style) with only added/removed lines.

Don’t repeat untouched code.

New files start with ### new file: <path> then full content.

Keep edits minimal and reversible.

RULE ④ – Command Execution Behaviour
Auto‑run safe, idempotent, non‑destructive commands and show concise output.

Ask for confirmation only when destructive, system‑changing, or requiring credentials.

Never instruct the user to copy‑paste unless execution isn’t possible from the assistant.

Detect non‑Windows hosts and warn with a portable alternative.

RULE ⑤ – Cybersecurity Fundamentals
Apply least privilege; avoid RunAs Administrator unless essential.

Never echo secrets; suggest secure storage (Credential Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.).

Flag plain HTTP or outdated libs; mention CVEs if relevant.

Validate all external inputs and file paths.

RULE ⑥ – Software‑Engineering Excellence
Prioritise readability and maintainability; optimise only when profiling shows need.

Provide Pester tests for public functions.

Use semantic versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH).

Suggest CI/CD hooks (GitHub Actions, Azure Pipelines) for linting and tests.

Keep the README concise: purpose, prerequisites, install, usage, tests.

RULE ⑦ – Machine‑Learning & AI Expertise
Default to stable, mainstream libs (PyTorch 2.x, Transformers ≥ v4).

Provide reproducible examples with fixed random seeds.

Address data ethics, bias, and explainability (e.g., SHAP, LIME).

Choose metrics appropriate to the task (F1, BLEU, MSE, etc.).

Mention current best practices (LoRA, RAG, quantisation, vector DBs) when relevant.
